= Solution
{parent=a}

A <non-Archimedean absolute value> on a field is a function $|\cdot|:K\to\mathbb R_{\ge0}$ with $|x|=0$ exactly for $x=0$, $|xy|=|x||y|$, and the <ultrametric inequality> $|x+y|\le\max(|x|,|y|)$. It is nontrivial if some nonzero element has value different from one. <Equivalent absolute values> induce the same topology; for nontrivial absolute values this is equivalent to $|x|_2=|x|_1^c$ for some $c>0$.

Here is also a proof of the latter equivalence in the case needed. A positive power plainly preserves the open neighborhoods of zero. Conversely, if the topologies coincide, then $|x|_1<1$ if and only if $x^n\to0$ in the first topology, hence if and only if $|x|_2<1$. Choose $a$ with $0<|a|_1<1$. For every $x\ne0$, $m\in\mathbb Z$ and $n\ge1$, whether $|x^n/a^m|<1$ agrees for the two values. Comparison with all rational numbers $m/n$ therefore identifies the two ratios $\log|x|_j/\log|a|_j$. Thus $|x|_2=|x|_1^c$ with $c=\log|a|_2/\log|a|_1>0$.

Now let $|\cdot|$ be nontrivial and non-Archimedean on $\mathbb Q$. Since $|1|=1$ and every positive integer is a sum of ones, $|n|\le1$ for every integer. If all nonzero integers had value one, multiplicativity would make the absolute value trivial on all rationals. Hence some integer has value less than one; its prime factorization shows that some prime $p$ has $|p|<1$.

There cannot be two distinct such primes $p,q$. A <Bézout identity> $ap+bq=1$ with $a,b\in\mathbb Z$ would give
$$
1=|ap+bq|\le\max(|a||p|,|b||q|)\le\max(|p|,|q|)<1.
$$
Thus every other prime has value one. Prime factorization of the numerator and denominator of $x\in\mathbb Q^\times$ now gives
$$
|x|=|p|^{v_p(x)}=p^{-c v_p(x)}=|x|_p^c,\qquad c=-\frac{\log|p|}{\log p}>0.
$$
Conversely $|x|_p=p^{-v_p(x)}$ is multiplicative and satisfies the <ultrametric inequality>, since $v_p(x+y)\ge\min(v_p(x),v_p(y))$. Distinct primes give inequivalent values: $p^m\to0$ for the $p$-adic absolute value but $|p^m|_q=1$ for $q\ne p$. We have proved the <non-Archimedean part of Ostrowski theorem>:
$$
\boxed{\text{The nontrivial non-Archimedean absolute values on }\mathbb Q\text{ are }|\cdot|_p^c,\quad p\text{ prime},\ c>0.}
$$
There is exactly one equivalence class for each prime.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A basis of open sets in the <p-adic integers> is
$$
\boxed{a+p^n\mathbb Z_p\qquad(a\in\mathbb Z_p,\ n\ge0).}
$$
Indeed this is the set of $x$ with $v_p(x-a)\ge n$, equivalently $|x-a|_p<p^{-(n-1)}$; these neighborhoods become arbitrarily small. Reduction identifies $\mathbb Z_p/p^n\mathbb Z_p$ with $\mathbb Z/p^n\mathbb Z$, so each has index $p^n$.

If a subgroup $H\le(\mathbb Z_p,+)$ is open, a neighborhood of zero of the displayed form lies inside $H$. Thus $p^n\mathbb Z_p\subseteq H$ for some $n$, and $[\mathbb Z_p:H]\le p^n$ is finite.

Conversely suppose the abstract index is the finite integer $m$. The quotient is a finite abelian group of order $m$, so Lagrange's theorem gives $mx\in H$ for every $x\in\mathbb Z_p$. Write $m=p^r u$ with $p\nmid u$. Multiplication by the <p-adic unit> $u$ is a bijection of $\mathbb Z_p$, its inverse being multiplication by $u^{-1}\in\mathbb Z_p$. Consequently
$$
p^r\mathbb Z_p=m\mathbb Z_p\subseteq H.
$$
The subgroup $H$ is a union of open cosets of $p^r\mathbb Z_p$, and is therefore open. This proves the characterization of <open subgroups of the p-adic integers>:
$$
\boxed{H\text{ open}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad[\mathbb Z_p:H]<\infty.}
$$
No prior assumption of closedness is required for the finite-index direction.

= 3
{parent=Paper 29}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For an odd integer $x=2a+1$, $x^2=1+4a(a+1)\equiv1\pmod8$. The same conclusion holds for every <2-adic unit> by reduction modulo eight. Thus an odd square must be congruent to one modulo eight.

Conversely let $u\in\mathbb Z_2^\times$ with $u\equiv1\pmod8$. For <digit lifting for square roots of 2-adic units>, start with $x_3=1$. If $x_n$ is odd and $x_n^2\equiv u\pmod{2^n}$, consider
$$
x_{n+1}=x_n+\varepsilon_n2^{n-1},\qquad\varepsilon_n\in\{0,1\}.
$$
For $n\ge3$,
$$
x_{n+1}^2\equiv x_n^2+\varepsilon_n2^nx_n\pmod{2^{n+1}},
$$
since the remaining term is divisible by $2^{2n-2}$ and $2n-2\ge n+1$. The odd multiplier $x_n$ lets the choice of $\varepsilon_n$ correct exactly the next binary digit. Thus $x_{n+1}^2\equiv u\pmod{2^{n+1}}$. The corrections have valuations tending to infinity, so completeness gives $x=\lim x_n\in\mathbb Z_2^\times$ with $x^2=u$.

The unit $3$ is congruent to one modulo two but is not a square, and $5$ is congruent to one modulo four but is not a square, by the necessary modulo-eight condition. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{The smallest }k\text{ is }3,\qquad u\text{ is a square}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ u\equiv1\pmod8.}
$$

Every nonzero <p-adic number> in $\mathbb Q_2$ is uniquely $2^m u$ with $m\in\mathbb Z$ and $u$ a <2-adic unit>. A square has even valuation, and the unit square criterion shows that the unit class is determined by its odd residue modulo eight. The four residues form $C_2\times C_2$, generated by $-1$ and $5$; the valuation parity supplies the third factor, generated by $2$. Hence the <square-class group of the 2-adic numbers> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb Q_2^\times/(\mathbb Q_2^\times)^2\cong C_2^3,\qquad\text{basis }[-1],[2],[5].}
$$
Its eight classes are represented by $1,-1,5,-5,2,-2,10,-10$. They are distinct: the first four have even valuation and distinct unit residues modulo eight, while multiplication by $2$ gives odd valuation and the same four distinct unit residues.

In characteristic different from two, completing the square in a quadratic minimal polynomial makes every quadratic extension $K(\sqrt d)$ for a nonsquare $d\in K$. If $K(\sqrt d)=K(\sqrt e)$, write $\sqrt e=a+b\sqrt d$ and square. The coefficient $2ab$ must vanish. Since $e$ is nonsquare, $b\ne0$, so $a=0$ and $e/d=b^2$. Conversely a square ratio plainly gives the same field. Thus the seven <quadratic extensions of the 2-adic numbers> are exactly
$$
\boxed{\mathbb Q_2(\sqrt{-1}),\ \mathbb Q_2(\sqrt5),\ \mathbb Q_2(\sqrt{-5}),\ \mathbb Q_2(\sqrt2),\ \mathbb Q_2(\sqrt{-2}),\ \mathbb Q_2(\sqrt{10}),\ \mathbb Q_2(\sqrt{-10}).}
$$

The unique <unramified extension> in this list is $\mathbb Q_2(\sqrt5)$. To verify this, $\alpha=(1+\sqrt5)/2$ is integral and satisfies $X^2-X-1=0$. Its reduction $X^2+X+1$ is irreducible over $\mathbb F_2$, so its residue field contains $\mathbb F_4$. The standard degree formula for finite extensions of complete discretely valued fields with perfect residue field is $[L:K]=e(L/K)f(L/K)$. Here the field degree is two, so the <residue degree> is two and the <ramification index> is one.

Conversely an unramified quadratic extension has residue field $\mathbb F_4$. A root there of $X^2+X+1$ lifts by <Hensel lemma> to a root of $X^2-X-1$, whose derivative is a unit. For this lift $\beta$, $(2\beta-1)^2=5$, so the extension contains $\mathbb Q_2(\sqrt5)$ and must equal it. Every other field in the list is therefore ramified.

For the requested <ramification groups>, choose $L=\mathbb Q_2(\sqrt2)$ and $\pi=\sqrt2$. We use uniqueness of the extension of the valuation to a finite extension, together with the degree formula just stated. Since $v_2(\pi)=1/2$, the extension has ramification index two and residue degree one. Normalize $v_L(\pi)=1$, giving $v_L(2)=2$. Every element is $a+b\pi$ with $a,b\in\mathbb Q_2$, and the two summands have valuations of opposite parity:
$$
v_L(a+b\pi)=\min\{2v_2(a),\ 2v_2(b)+1\}.
$$
Thus $a+b\pi$ is integral exactly when $a,b\in\mathbb Z_2$, proving $\mathcal O_L=\mathbb Z_2\oplus\mathbb Z_2\pi$ directly.

The nonidentity automorphism $\sigma$ negates $\pi$. For any integral $a+b\pi$,
$$
\sigma(a+b\pi)-(a+b\pi)=-2b\pi,
$$
of valuation at least three, with equality when $b$ is a unit. By the definition $G_i=\{\sigma:v_L(\sigma x-x)\ge i+1\text{ for every }x\in\mathcal O_L\}$, the <ramification in the quadratic extension generated by the square root of two> is therefore
$$
\boxed{G_1=G_2=\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q_2)\cong C_2,\qquad G_i=1\text{ for every }i\ge3.}
$$
The lower ramification break is two; the valuation used here is the integral normalization on $L$.

